---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import ServicesRelatedWork from "../../components/ServicesRelatedWork.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Ongoing Design, Web &amp; Marketing Retainers | Kelp"
  description="Keep design, development, and marketing moving with a monthly or weekly Kelp retainer built around your priorities."
>
  <SimpleHero
    tagline="Ongoing Support Services"
    title="Ongoing Retainers"
    description="Some businesses do not need one more one-off project. They need a reliable creative and digital partner who can keep work moving every week or every month."
    lottieFile="ongoing-retainers.json"
  />
  <div class="service-body">
    <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <h2>Where teams get stuck</h2>
      <p>
        After a launch or initial project, many businesses still need regular
        design, web, or marketing support. The work keeps coming, but the
        internal bandwidth is inconsistent and one-off projects slow everything
        down.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is usually when a retainer starts making more sense than starting
        from zero each time.
      </p>
      <h2>How we fix it</h2>
      <p>
        A retainer gives you structured access to Kelp so priorities can keep
        moving without rebuilding the relationship, scope, and process every few
        weeks.
      </p>
      <h2>Your options</h2>
      <h3 class="h6">Monthly Retainer</h3>
      <p>
        At the beginning of each month, Kelp is assigned a set number of
        production hours to accomplish the agreed upon tasks. This works well
        for teams that have a clearer roadmap and need reliable capacity to keep
        planned work moving.
      </p>
      <h3 class="h6">Weekly Retainer</h3>
      <p>
        These retainers are treated as weekly sprints where each week has a set
        number of production hours and a tighter feedback loop. They work best
        for fast-moving workloads that need consistent attention and regular
        prioritization.
      </p>
      <h2>Choose the Cadence Around the Work</h2>
      <p>
        A monthly allocation suits a planned roadmap; weekly sprints suit faster
        decisions and a tighter feedback loop. We agree on priorities, capacity,
        owners, and a review rhythm so requests do not disappear into an
        open-ended queue. The work may cross design, development, marketing, and
        support as the business needs change.
      </p>
      <h2>Keep Improvement Measurable</h2>
      <p>
        We review what was completed, what the team learned, and what should
        happen next. For website and commerce clients, that can include content,
        conversion, performance, and integrations as well as day-to-day support.
        Plan ongoing support with Kelp.
      </p>
      <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        A retainer is most useful when the work changes month to month but the
        need for an accountable team remains. We agree on priorities, decision
        makers, response expectations, and how new requests enter the queue.
        Regular planning keeps urgent maintenance from consuming every hour and
        leaves room for improvements to content, conversion paths, and
        integrations that compound over time.
      </p>
      <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Recurring planning and prioritization</li>
        <li>Defined production capacity</li>
        <li>Cross-discipline design, web, or marketing work</li>
        <li>Progress review and next-cycle recommendations</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the work your team expects over the next quarter and the
        speed at which decisions need to happen. We can recommend a cadence and
        capacity that match the volume.
      </p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Is a retainer the right fit for one project?</h3>
      <p>
        Usually a defined project is clearer for a single outcome. A retainer
        suits ongoing or changing priorities that need dependable capacity.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
          Plan Ongoing Support
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ServicesRelatedWork tag={80} />
  </div>
  <FeaturedArticles />
</Layout>
